#pragma once
#ifndef HECKE_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION_H
#define HECKE_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION_H

// Stage S10 -- level-(c) block-diagonalization, single-operator case.
//
// BINDING DEFINITION (plan Sec. 3, "Level (c) -- block-diagonalized level"):
// the block-diagonalization of H^q under the commutative F_ell-algebra T
// generated by the computed Hecke matrices is the PRIMARY DECOMPOSITION of
// H^q as a T-module,
//
//     H^q = (+)_{m in MaxSpec(T)} H^q[m^infty],
//
// one block per maximal ideal m, together with an adapted basis U so that
// U^{-1} A^{(q)}(T_p) U is block-diagonal. This is correct over the
// NON-algebraically-closed field F_ell: no eigenvalues are ever extracted
// into an extension field -- each block carries only the irreducible factor
// pi in F_ell[X], its nilpotency data (min poly pi^e, generalized-Jordan
// partition), and the residue degree f = deg pi. Non-semisimplicity is
// exhibited exactly when some e > 1 (equivalently, some partition is not
// (1,1,...,1)).
//
// STAGE S10 implements the SINGLE-OPERATOR case k = 1: `ops` is exactly one
// pair (p, A^{(q)}(T_p)); the block decomposition is then the primary
// decomposition of the single matrix A: charpoly(A) = prod pi_j^{m_j}
// (factored by the certified F_ell[X] toolkit of FpPoly.h), primary
// components ker pi_j(A)^{m_j}, per-component generalized-Jordan partitions
// from the ranks of pi_j(A)^r (conjugate-partition arithmetic), and a
// within-block cyclic-vector / quotient-chain basis bringing each block to
// primary rational canonical form (companion blocks of pi^s).
//
// STAGE S11 implements the SIMULTANEOUS refinement for a commuting family
// k > 1: `ops` is a family of pairs (p_j, A^{(q)}(T_{p_j})), all pairwise
// commuting (the exact-solve invariance certificate below IS the runtime
// commutation check), same degree q. Starting from the whole space as one
// block, each operator in turn refines every current block via its OWN
// primary decomposition (exact solve `basis * X = A_{p_j} * basis` restricts
// A_{p_j} to the block; solvability certifies invariance = commutation with
// everything used to build the block so far); sub-blocks are recorded by
// composing bases. A subspace of a pi-primary space stays pi-primary under
// any further invariant-subspace refinement (Cayley-Hamilton: its charpoly
// under any earlier operator divides that operator's power of the SAME
// irreducible), so one pass through the family already reaches the
// MaxSpec(T) fixed point OF THE PER-OPERATOR REFINEMENT; compute() nonetheless
// repeats full passes, defensively, until a pass causes no further splitting
// (capped at d_q + |family| + 2 passes -- termination is guaranteed because
// block dimensions strictly drop on any pass that does split). That fixed
// point is NOT yet the MaxSpec(T) decomposition: a block on which no single
// operator acts primarily may still carry two maximal ideals ([E] Claim 3.1),
// and the LOCALITY STAGE below (hecke-extensions phase E0b) is what decides
// it -- certifying the block local, or splitting it by a certified witness.
// Once the fixed point is reached, every block's char/min-poly/Jordan-partition data for
// EVERY operator in the family is (re)computed directly by restricting that
// operator's matrix to the (basis-independent) final block subspace --
// consistent with (and, for k = 1, IDENTICAL in content to) the S10
// per-operator data.
//
// RESIDUE DEGREE (S11, decisive & exact; plan Sec. 3 / amendment A-N4): for
// a block, f_j := deg(pi_j) for every operator p_j in the family (pi_j the
// block's irreducible factor for p_j); f := lcm_j(f_j) divides
// [T/m : F_ell]. Equality is CERTIFIED by exhibiting a witness element
// (either a single T_{p_j} with f_j == f, or -- deterministic-seed search,
// bounded budget -- a linear combination sum c_j T_{p_j}) whose min-poly
// squarefree part has a single irreducible factor of degree EXACTLY f,
// verified by the deterministic CertifyIrreducible machinery (randomization
// only ever proposes a witness; it never certifies one). When a witness is
// found: `PrimaryBlock::residue_degree = f` and `residue_degree_certified =
// true`, with the witness (prime, coefficient) pairs stored in
// `residue_witness` for O(1) independent re-verification by certify(). When
// no witness is found within budget: `residue_degree` still holds the
// PROVEN LOWER BOUND f (the interval is [f, dim_block]) but
// `residue_degree_certified = false` -- an unproven degree is NEVER reported
// as the (unqualified) "residue_degree" key in to_json() (it is reported as
// "residue_degree_lower_bound" instead; see A-N4 / Hecke/README.md).
//
// MULTIPLICITY (S11 risk, binding documentation requirement; REWRITTEN in
// hecke-extensions phase E0b, slot 2 WP-2 -- the pre-E0b text made two false
// claims, [I G1]): a block with dim_block > deg_block * f (residue_degree) is
// an ISOTYPIC 𝕋-primary block of that multiplicity OVER THE COMPUTED FAMILY.
// That is a statement about the family 𝕋 = F_ell[T_{p_1}, ..., T_{p_k}] that
// was actually computed, and nothing more:
//   * it is NOT a claim of irreducibility. An isotypic block of multiplicity
//     m > 1 is, when semisimple, a direct sum of m copies of one simple
//     module; "irreducible" is false for every m > 1;
//   * it is NOT a claim that no Hecke operator separates it. Adding an
//     operator OUTSIDE the computed family routinely refines a block -- most
//     obviously for k = 1, where a single T_p decides nothing beyond its own
//     primary decomposition.
// What IS true, and what the certificates say: after the locality stage every
// emitted block with local_certified = true is ONE maximal ideal of the
// computed 𝕋 ([E] Cert. 5.2), so no F_ell-linear combination of the computed
// operators refines it; the cuspidal 2-dimensional block of a weight-k
// level-1 eigenform stays together under every T_p (holomorphic and
// antiholomorphic share the full eigensystem, multiplicity 2) -- a fact about
// modular forms, not a consequence of this algorithm. Do not chase phantom
// splittings within the computed family; report the multiplicity, the
// semisimplicity and the family size honestly (multiplicity_note).
//
// CERTIFICATION (binding; plan Sec. 3 "Certification"): certify() re-derives
// every claim deterministically and unconditionally, independently of the
// randomized factor SEARCH:
//   * det U != 0 (fresh inverse + U * U^{-1} == I),
//   * U^{-1} A_{p_j} U exactly block-diagonal with the reported block sizes,
//     for EVERY p_j in the family,
//   * per block, per p_j: the block's characteristic polynomial (recomputed
//     by the division-free Berkowitz algorithm on the conjugated diagonal
//     block) equals the reported pi^m; pi re-extracted by the deterministic
//     squarefree machinery and re-certified irreducible (gcd/Frobenius
//     proof, CertifyIrreducible); pi(A|block)^e = 0 and pi(A|block)^{e-1} != 0
//     for the reported min-poly exponent e; the reported Jordan partition
//     re-derived from the ranks of pi(A|block)^r on the block,
//   * per block: the reported residue degree equals the honestly recomputed
//     lcm_j(deg pi_j); if reported certified, the stored witness combination
//     is independently re-evaluated on the block and re-verified to have a
//     squarefree min-poly part with a single irreducible factor of exactly
//     that degree,
//   * sum of block dims = d, and prod of the reported block char polys =
//     charpoly(A_{p_j}) recomputed on the ORIGINAL matrix, for every p_j (the
//     factorization certificate).
// Randomization (Cantor-Zassenhaus inside FactorPoly, and the witness search)
// is only ever used to FIND factors/witnesses; FactorPoly itself certifies
// before returning, and certify() re-verifies from the reported data alone.
//
// JSON: to_json() follows the plan Sec. 3 schema; polynomial coefficient
// lists are LOW -> HIGH degree and matrices are arrays of ROWS with entries
// in [0, ell) (amendment A-N4, see Hecke/README.md). Its schema string is
// `hecke_module_decomposition/v2` since hecke-extensions phase E0b (slot 2
// WP-2), which added the three locality keys `local`, `witness_poly` and
// `coefficient_field` and corrected the multiplicity note. THIS OBJECT IS
// HASHED NOWHERE ([X] Sec. 1.6, [XA M1]): the golden `report_*` texts are
// written by Hecke/HeckeReport.cpp, which carries its own schema
// (`hecke_report/v2`).
//
// Build placement (documented per plan stage S10): compiled into
// cohomology_app, NOT into the pure-integer HeckeCore static library,
// because it consumes ModArithmetic's Matrix (kernel / rank / inverse /
// RREF). Stage S10 is mathematically independent of the Hecke pipeline
// S1-S9: it operates on given F_ell matrices.

#include "FpPoly.h"
#include "HeckeSettings.h"

#include "../../ModArithmetic/Matrix.h"

#include <cstdint>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <utility>
#include <vector>

namespace hecke {

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (hecke-extensions phase E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) WHICH PHASE OF THE LOCALITY
// ROUTE REACHED THE VERDICT ([X] Sec. 3.1 step 4, [E] Cert. `sec:localcert`
// "Strict mode": `phase_reached in {linear, berlekamp, residue_search}`).
//
//   Linear         -- the bounded LINEAR witness family sum_p c_p t_p decided
//                     the block.  This is the ONLY phase budget mode has, and
//                     it is the phase every block of the golden grid reaches,
//                     in either mode.
//   Berlekamp      -- the linear family was exhausted and step 2 (dim B >= 2)
//                     SPLIT the block.  A block that reaches it disappears, so
//                     this value survives only on the STATE (the high-water
//                     mark of the whole run), never on a surviving block.
//   ResidueSearch  -- the linear family was exhausted, dim B = 1 proved the
//                     block local, and the witness came from Abar's own
//                     coordinate vectors.
//
// The order is monotone: LocalityState::phase_reached is the maximum over the
// run, and a block's own phase_reached is where ITS verdict was reached.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
enum class LocalityPhase : uint32_t { Linear = 0, Berlekamp = 1, ResidueSearch = 2 };
const char* to_string(LocalityPhase ph);

struct PrimaryBlock {
    uint32_t dim, residue_degree;
    Matrix basis_columns;                              // d x dim; columns span the block
    std::map<uint64_t, FpPoly> min_poly, char_poly;    // per Hecke prime p
    std::map<uint64_t, std::vector<uint32_t>> jordan_partition; // per p; partition of dim/deg(pi_p), parts descending

    // Stage S11 additions (binding: A-N4 residue-degree honesty; see the
    // header comment above). `residue_degree` ALWAYS holds f = lcm_j(f_j)
    // (for a single-operator block, k = 1, this is simply deg(pi) -- exactly
    // the S10 semantics, unchanged). `residue_degree_certified` records
    // whether a witness proving f is the TRUE residue degree (rather than
    // merely a proven lower bound) was found; `residue_witness` is that
    // witness, a family of (p, coefficient) pairs with nonzero coefficients
    // (coefficient taken mod ell) such that sum coefficient * T_p, restricted
    // to this block, has a squarefree min-poly part with a single
    // irreducible factor of degree EXACTLY `residue_degree`. Populated (and,
    // for k = 1, always trivially certified with the single-element witness
    // {(p, 1)}) by every compute() path -- never left at its default when a
    // block exists.
    bool residue_degree_certified = false;
    std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, uint32_t>> residue_witness;

    // ---- (hecke-extensions phase E0b, slot 2 WP-1) THE LOCALITY CERTIFICATE.
    //
    // `residue_degree_certified` above and `local_certified` here are TWO
    // DIFFERENT CLAIMS and must not be conflated:
    //   * residue_degree_certified -- "f = lcm_j deg(pi_j) really is the
    //     residue field degree", witnessed by `residue_witness` passing
    //     clause (1) of [E] Cert. 5.2 alone (an irreducible squarefree
    //     char-poly part of degree exactly f).  UNCHANGED semantics: it is
    //     still set by the FIRST candidate of the search that passes clause
    //     (1), exactly as the pre-E0b code did.
    //   * local_certified -- "this block really is ONE maximal ideal", i.e.
    //     [E] Cert. 5.2 in FULL: pi_w irreducible of degree f, the
    //     Teichmueller lift w_hat = w^(ell^N) satisfying pi_w(w_hat) = 0, and
    //     a character P_p of degree < f with t_p - P_p(w_hat) NILPOTENT for
    //     every operator of the family.  Clause (1) alone does NOT imply it
    //     ([E] Claim 3.1), which is the defect E0b repairs.
    //
    // `compute()` now accepts a block only on the full certificate; a
    // candidate with >= 2 distinct irreducible factors SPLITS the block
    // instead, and a block whose budget ([X] Sec. 1.5 `locality`) runs out is
    // emitted `local_certified = false` with `residue_degree` as a proven
    // LOWER BOUND -- never silently, and the loop never spins.
    //
    // For k = 1 the block is local for free (F_ell[t]/(pi^e) is a local ring),
    // so this is filled ANALYTICALLY and `w_hat` is never formed ([X] Sec. 3.1,
    // [XA m17]): witness_poly = pi, w_hat_exponent = N, character = {p -> X
    // mod pi_w} (the REDUCED representative of degree < f -- the constant
    // alpha at f = 1).
    bool local_certified = false;
    FpPoly witness_poly;                    // pi_w; empty iff !local_certified
    uint32_t w_hat_exponent = 0;            // N: least multiple of f with ell^N >= dim
    std::map<uint64_t, FpPoly> character;   // P_p, deg < f, one per family prime

    // (E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) STRICT MODE'S WITNESS, and the phase that found it.
    //
    // A LINEAR witness is reported in `residue_witness` as (p, c_p) pairs, and
    // that is all budget mode ever produces.  Strict mode's step-3 witness is
    // drawn from the WHOLE residue field Abar = A_S / rad A_S -- it is a
    // polynomial in the t_p, not an F_ell-combination of them -- so it is
    // reported as the matrix w|_block itself.  EXACTLY ONE of the two is
    // non-empty on a block flagged `local_certified`, and certify() rebuilds
    // the witness from whichever it is (codes 25 and 30).
    //
    // `witness_matrix` is EMPTY on every block budget mode produces, which is
    // why no golden object moves: the golden hashes read `residue_witness`,
    // `residue_degree*`, the polynomial data and `U` only.
    Matrix witness_matrix;
    LocalityPhase phase_reached = LocalityPhase::Linear;
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (hecke-extensions phase E0b, slot 2 WP-1) THE RESUMABLE LOCALITY STATE
// ([X] Sec. 1.3.1 R3.3 item 2, verbatim member list).
//
// The locality loop is one of the six budgeted computations of R3: a budget
// can cut it off with an incomplete result (a block whose locality is neither
// certified nor refuted), so it stores its iteration counts and its current
// partition and can be continued with a higher budget.
//
// WHY THE CONTINUATION IS A PREFIX CUT (resume-invariance, R3.1 item 3).  The
// witness candidates of a block form ONE fixed sequence, a function of
// (dim, ell, kResidueWitnessSeed, attempt) alone:
//     [0 .. m-1]  every operator whose deg(pi_p) == f, in the family's order,
//                 as the trivial witness {(p, 1)};
//     [m]         the all-ones combination;
//     [m+1 ..]    the deterministic-seed combinations.
// The budget decides WHERE that sequence stops, never what it does; and a
// split restarts the whole stage on the new partition, at attempt 0 for every
// block, whichever run produced it.  So run(b1) + resume(b2) == run(b2)
// entrywise, modulo `budget_history` -- which R3.1 item 4 makes [b1, b2]
// against [b2] and which is therefore the one member that MUST differ.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct LocalityBlockState {
    uint32_t dim = 0;
    Matrix basis_columns;         // the block's columns, as in PrimaryBlock

    // `local == false` means UNKNOWN under the budget in force (never "proved
    // non-local": a non-local block SPLITS and so never survives the loop).
    bool local = false;
    uint32_t attempts = 0;        // candidates consumed from the fixed sequence
    uint32_t residue_degree_lower_bound = 0;   // f = lcm_j deg(pi_j)

    // The certificate, where local.  Mirrors PrimaryBlock's four fields.
    FpPoly witness_poly;
    uint32_t w_hat_exponent = 0;
    std::map<uint64_t, FpPoly> character;
    std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, uint32_t>> witness;

    // Auditability ([X] R3.3 item 2: "and, for auditability, the c vectors
    // tried").  One entry per consumed candidate, in order.
    std::vector<std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, uint32_t>>> combos_tried;

    // The residue-degree claim reached so far, carried across a resume so that
    // the continuation reproduces a fresh run exactly (the shipped semantics:
    // the FIRST candidate passing clause (1) wins).
    bool residue_degree_certified = false;
    std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, uint32_t>> residue_witness;

    // (E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) Mirrors PrimaryBlock's two strict-mode fields.
    Matrix witness_matrix;
    LocalityPhase phase_reached = LocalityPhase::Linear;

    bool equals(const LocalityBlockState& o) const;
};

struct LocalityState {
    LocalityMode mode = LocalityMode::Budget;
    uint32_t budget = kDefaultLocalityBudget;
    std::vector<uint32_t> budget_history;   // one entry per run()/resume()

    std::vector<LocalityBlockState> blocks; // the current partition, in block order
    uint32_t splits = 0;                    // how often a candidate split a block
    bool complete = true;                   // every block certified local

    // (E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) The HIGH-WATER phase of the whole run, split
    // parents included ([X] Sec. 3.1 step 4), and whether a cooperative
    // cancellation cut the run off at a step boundary (R3.3 item 5 -- the ONE
    // way strict mode leaves a block `local = false`).  `interrupted` is
    // recomputed by every run, so a resume that completes clears it.
    LocalityPhase phase_reached = LocalityPhase::Linear;
    bool interrupted = false;

    // Entrywise equality.  `ignore_budget_history` is what resume-invariance
    // needs (see the comment above; the SturmState::equals convention).
    bool equals(const LocalityState& o, bool ignore_budget_history) const;

    // Fixed key order, no pointers, no hash containers (R3.1 item 1).
    std::string to_json() const;
};

class HeckeModuleDecomposition {
public:
    // ops: pairs (p, A^{(q)}(T_p)) over F_ell, all pairwise commuting (family
    // sizes k >= 1). k = 1 is the S10 single-operator primary decomposition;
    // k > 1 is the S11 simultaneous MaxSpec(T) refinement.
    HeckeModuleDecomposition(std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, Matrix>> ops, uint32_t ell);

    // Runs the primary decomposition (k = 1, Stage S10) or the simultaneous
    // MaxSpec(T) refinement (k > 1, Stage S11). Idempotent. Throws
    // std::runtime_error / std::invalid_argument on any internal exactness
    // assertion failure (including an exact-solve failure during refinement,
    // which certifies that some pair of supplied operators does NOT
    // commute), and std::runtime_error if the defensive refinement pass cap
    // is exhausted without reaching a fixed point (k > 1 only).
    void compute();

    const std::vector<PrimaryBlock>& blocks() const;

    // (hecke-extensions E0a) The family this object was built from, and its
    // modulus -- read-only, so that the free function CertifyLocality below can
    // be run over an already-computed decomposition without rebuilding it.
    const std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, Matrix>>& ops() const;
    uint32_t ell() const;

    // U: columns are the concatenated block bases, in block order.
    const Matrix& change_of_basis() const;

    // U^{-1} A^{(q)}(T_p) U, verified exactly block-diagonal with the
    // reported block sizes (throws otherwise).
    Matrix conjugated(uint64_t p) const;

    // Unconditional certificate: 0 iff ALL the plan-Sec.-3 certificates pass
    // (re-derived independently of the search; see the header comment).
    // Nonzero return values identify the first failing certificate.
    uint32_t certify() const;

    // ---- (hecke-extensions phase E0b, slot 2 WP-1) the locality loop's
    // resumable state, and its continuation under a higher budget.
    //
    // `locality_state()` is available after compute(), complete or not.
    // `resume_locality(N)` continues from it ([X] Sec. 1.3.1 R3.1 item 2):
    //   * a budget BELOW the one already reached is refused with a named
    //     error ("resume: budget below reached state");
    //   * on a complete state it is a strict no-op -- neither the budget nor
    //     the history moves, so the decomposition is literally unchanged;
    //   * otherwise every block whose locality is still unknown continues at
    //     its stored attempt index, and a split restarts the stage on the new
    //     partition.
    // After it, blocks(), change_of_basis(), conjugated() and certify() all
    // describe the CONTINUED decomposition.
    const LocalityState& locality_state() const;
    void resume_locality(uint32_t new_budget);

    // TEST-ONLY (slot 3 WP-3): mutable access to the computed blocks, so that
    // a validation can CORRUPT the reported certificate data and prove that
    // certify() rejects it with the right code.  Nothing in the pipeline, the
    // golden harness, --bench or the campaign calls it; it exists for
    // HeckeLocalityValidation's tamper arm, the way BarCocycleSolverValidation
    // keeps a frozen solver body.
    std::vector<PrimaryBlock>& blocks_for_tamper_test();

    std::string to_string_offset(uint32_t offset) const;
    std::string to_json() const;

private:
    std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, Matrix>> ops_;
    uint32_t ell_;
    uint32_t d_ = 0;
    bool computed_ = false;
    std::vector<PrimaryBlock> blocks_;
    Matrix U_, U_inv_;
    LocalityState loc_;

    const Matrix& op_matrix(uint64_t p) const;
    void require_computed(const char* who) const;

    // (E0b) The post-fixed-point locality stage of [X] Sec. 3.1 steps 1-3.
    // `bases` is the current partition (each entry d x dim); `carried` is the
    // per-block state a resume continues from (empty on a fresh run).  It
    // fills blocks_ and loc_.blocks, restarting itself whenever a candidate
    // splits a block.
    void RunLocalityStage(std::vector<Matrix>& bases,
                          std::vector<LocalityBlockState> carried,
                          uint32_t budget);
    // (E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) The TERMINATING route of [X] Sec. 3.1 steps 1-3,
    // entered ONLY in strict mode and ONLY after the linear family of
    // `budget` candidates has been exhausted without a certificate and
    // without a split -- which is why strict mode is byte-identical to budget
    // mode wherever budget mode certifies.  Either fills `st` with a
    // certificate (returns true, `split_bases` untouched) or fills
    // `split_bases` with the sub-blocks of the Berlekamp split in the BLOCK's
    // own coordinates (returns false).  Never searches a linear witness.
    // Returns kStrictCertified (0), kStrictSplit (1) or kStrictInterrupted (2).
    uint32_t StrictLocalityRoute(const PrimaryBlock& block,
                                 const std::map<uint64_t, FpPoly>& pi_by_p,
                                 const std::map<uint64_t, Matrix>& Asub_by_p,
                                 uint32_t f_lcm, uint32_t budget,
                                 LocalityBlockState& st,
                                 std::vector<Matrix>& split_bases);
    // The op-by-op refinement passes, to the MaxSpec(T) fixed point.
    void RefineToFixedPoint(std::vector<Matrix>& bases) const;
    // Everything the U_ / U_inv_ / block-diagonality tail does, shared by
    // compute() and resume_locality().
    void FinalizeChangeOfBasis();

    // Stage S10: k = 1 primary decomposition (unchanged body from S10, moved
    // verbatim into this method so the S11 dispatch in compute() cannot
    // regress it).
    void ComputeSingleOp();

    // Stage S11: k > 1 simultaneous MaxSpec(T) refinement (see the header
    // comment for the algorithm).
    void ComputeMultiOp();
};

// ===========================================================================
// (hecke-extensions phase E0a) READ-ONLY locality certificate.
// ===========================================================================
//
// THE DEFECT IT DECIDES.  ComputeMultiOp() stops refining when every operator
// of the family has a SINGLE irreducible factor on a block, and then certifies
// the residue degree by a witness w whose char-poly squarefree part is ONE
// irreducible of degree f = lcm_j deg(pi_j).  That predicate does NOT imply
// that the block is local (i.e. that the algebra T_S it carries has a single
// maximal ideal): [E] Claim 3.1 exhibits t_1 = (alpha, alpha^ell),
// t_2 = (alpha, alpha) on F_{ell^2}^2, where every operator has the single
// irreducible min-poly pi and the witness w = t_1 is accepted, yet
// T_S / rad = F_{ell^2} x F_{ell^2} -- two maximal ideals, so the reported
// "block" is really two.
//
// WHAT CERTIFIES LOCALITY ([E] Cert. 5.2, BOTH clauses).  With D = dim block:
//   (1) pi_w := the squarefree part of charpoly(w) is irreducible of degree
//       exactly f;
//   (2) N := the least multiple of f with ell^N >= D, and
//       w_hat := w^(ell^N) is the Teichmueller lift of w's residue ([E] Claim
//       3.3: w = s + n with n^D = 0, so w^(ell^N) = s^(ell^N) = s by Frobenius
//       plus s^(ell^f) = s); pi_w(w_hat) = 0 is checked, not assumed;
//   (3) for every operator t_p of the family there is P_p in F_ell[X] with
//       deg P_p < f such that n_p := t_p - P_p(w_hat) is NILPOTENT.
// Clause (3) is what Claim 3.1's pair fails: no such P_2 exists.
//
// HOW P_p IS FOUND (and why finding it is only a search).  On
//     U := ker pi_p(t_p) INTERSECT ker pi_w(w)
// -- which contains the socle of a local block, hence is nonzero there -- t_p
// and P_p(w_hat) are commuting semisimple operators differing by a nilpotent,
// so they agree; and 1, w_hat, ..., w_hat^{f-1} are independent on U, so the
// exact solve sum_i c_i (w_hat|_U)^i = t_p|_U has at most one solution.  The
// CERTIFICATE is clause (3) on the WHOLE block, re-derived from the returned
// (pi_w, N, P_p) alone -- the solve is never trusted by itself.
//
// k = 1 (a single operator).  F_ell[t]/(pi^e) is local for free, so nothing is
// computed: pi_w = pi, P = X mod pi_w, n = t - w_hat, and w_hat -- which would
// cost N*ceil(log2 ell) D x D products on EVERY single-operator decomposition
// of the campaign, the golden harness and `--bench decomp` -- is never formed.
// LocalityOptions::force_matrix_path turns the shortcut off so that
// HeckeLocalityValidation can compare the two paths.
//
// READ-ONLY IN E0a.  compute() never calls this; nothing in the pipeline does.
// It exists so that the fix's verdict can be taken over every existing
// decomposition (GoldenLocalitySweep) BEFORE the golden reference is touched.
// E0b is what moves the call inside the refinement loop.

struct LocalityVerdict {
    bool local = false;              // 0 == failure and the certificate holds
    uint32_t failure = 0;            // see LocalityFailure below; 0 iff local
    uint32_t residue_degree = 0;     // f = lcm_j deg(pi_j), re-derived here
    FpPoly witness_poly;             // pi_w (empty when no witness was accepted)
    uint32_t w_hat_exponent = 0;     // N
    std::map<uint64_t, FpPoly> character;  // P_p, deg < f, one per family prime
    std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, uint32_t>> witness;  // the accepted (p, c_p)
    bool analytic = false;           // the k = 1 shortcut was taken
    uint32_t attempts = 0;           // witness candidates consumed
    Matrix w_hat;                    // EMPTY on the analytic path (never formed)
    Matrix witness_matrix;           // w restricted to the block; empty if analytic
    std::string note;                // human-readable reason when !local
};

// Failure codes (stable; E0b's certify() codes 25-29 are derived from these).
enum LocalityFailure : uint32_t {
    kLocalityOk = 0,
    kLocalityNoWitness = 1,        // budget exhausted, no admissible pi_w
    kLocalityWHatNotInResidue = 2, // pi_w(w_hat) != 0
    kLocalityEmptyIntersection = 3,// ker pi_p(t_p) & ker pi_w(w) = 0
    kLocalityCharacterUnsolvable = 4, // the P_p system is inconsistent
    kLocalityNotNilpotent = 5,     // t_p - P_p(w_hat) is not nilpotent
    kLocalityDegreeMismatch = 6,   // the re-derived f disagrees with the block's
    kLocalityMalformedBlock = 7    // char/min-poly data missing or not primary
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// (hecke-extensions phase E0b-S, slot 3 WP-3) THE COOPERATIVE CANCELLATION
// HOOK ([X] Sec. 1.3.1 R3.3 item 5, [X] Sec. 3.1 step 4).
//
// TEST-ONLY.  The limit is 0 (off) unless a validation sets it, and nothing
// in the pipeline, the golden harness, --bench or the campaign ever sets it.
// It is thread_local, like the settings themselves.
//
// A "step boundary" is between two witness candidates (linear or residue) and
// between two splits -- NEVER inside an elimination, which is what makes the
// interrupted state exactly a PREFIX state and the resume invariant (R3.1
// item 3).  When the limit fires, every block whose locality is still unknown
// is emitted with `local = false`, the LocalityState carries
// `interrupted = true` / `complete = false`, and `resume_locality()` continues
// from the stored attempt indices to the same partition and the same
// certificates as an uninterrupted run.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The three outcomes of the strict route (see StrictLocalityRoute).
enum StrictLocalityOutcome : uint32_t {
    kStrictCertified = 0,
    kStrictSplit = 1,
    kStrictInterrupted = 2
};

void SetLocalityStepLimit(uint32_t limit);     // 0 = no limit (the default)
uint32_t LocalityStepLimit();
uint32_t LocalityStepsTaken();                 // steps consumed since the last reset
void ResetLocalitySteps();

// TEST-ONLY (slot 3 WP-3): how many SEEDED candidates strict mode's step-3
// residue search tries before falling back to the deterministic lexicographic
// enumeration of Abar's coordinate vectors.  The default, kStrictSeededFromBudget,
// means "the locality budget in force" -- which is what ships, and under which
// the fallback is never entered (expected trials ~ 1).  A validation sets it to
// 0 to reach the fallback on a small constructed case.
inline constexpr uint32_t kStrictSeededFromBudget = 0xFFFFFFFFu;
void SetStrictResidueSeededTrials(uint32_t n);
uint32_t StrictResidueSeededTrials();

struct LocalityOptions {
    uint32_t max_attempts = 0;      // 0 = the shipped kResidueWitnessMaxAttempts
    bool force_matrix_path = false; // run the general path even when k == 1
};

// Runs the certificate of [E] Cert. 5.2 over one already-computed block of a
// decomposition of `ops` over F_ell.  Pure: it mutates nothing and throws only
// on a malformed argument (a wrong modulus or a non-square operator); every
// mathematical failure is reported through the verdict.
LocalityVerdict CertifyLocality(const PrimaryBlock& block,
                                const std::vector<std::pair<uint64_t, Matrix>>& ops,
                                uint32_t ell,
                                const LocalityOptions& opts = LocalityOptions());

} // namespace hecke

#endif // HECKE_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION_H
